Title:
Bypassing EDR with Local AI

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post summarizes a lab experiment testing whether LLMs can generate an LSASS credential dumper that avoids modern EDR detection with minimal operator guidance. Hosted Claude models refused outright, while an open-weight DeepSeek model produced a functional LSASS minidump/XOR-to-disk tool that was still detected and then hit a “stealth” guardrail. An uncensored local Qwen 3.8 27B model (run on 2× RTX 4090) iteratively modified the tool (process spawning, reduced access masks, random sleeps, output path/name changes, and string scrubbing) until two unnamed lab EDRs did not alert. The key point is not a new dumping primitive, but that local/uncensored models can rapidly generate unique variants that sidestep signature-leaning detections. It’s useful for red teams as a “variant compiler” for bespoke tooling, and for defenders as a warning that model policy controls don’t constrain attacker-side local weights. The defensive emphasis is on least privilege, Credential Guard/PPL where possible, and behavior-based hunting around LSASS access and dump-like write patterns rather than static indicators.

Technical Focus:
- Windows LSASS credential dumping (MITRE ATT&CK T1003.001)
- Minidump generation and process cloning/reflection patterns
- EDR evasion via variant generation (string scrubbing, path changes, jitter/sleeps)
- Handle access mask minimization vs PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS
- Local/uncensored LLMs vs hosted-model guardrails and policy enforcement
- Behavioral detection ideas for dump-sized writes and suspicious LSASS access

Use Cases:
- Rapid generation and mutation of post-exploitation tooling to test EDR coverage in a lab
- Purple-team validation of LSASS access/dump detections beyond known Mimikatz signatures
- Developing behavior-based hunts for clone/minidump + encrypted-on-disk artifacts
- Threat modeling attacker capability using local open-weight/uncensored LLMs

Keywords:
LSASS, credential dumping, T1003.001, EDR evasion, local LLM, uncensored model, Qwen 3.8 27B, DeepSeek v4, Claude, minidump, pypykatz, Mimikatz, OpenProcess, access mask, PROCESS_ALL_ACCESS, string obfuscation, XOR encryption, process cloning, behavioral detection, Credential Guard, PPL, RunAsPPL, Windows internals